# Multi-Agent Debugging System — Run Instructions (Groq)

## 1. Install dependencies
```
pip install groq pytest
```
No mutmut needed — mutation testing now uses a built-in pure-Python engine
(simple_mutation_test.py) that works natively on Windows.

## 2. Get a Groq API key
https://console.groq.com/keys (free tier available)

## 3. Set the key
Windows PowerShell:
```
$env:GROQ_API_KEY = "gsk_..."
```

## 4. Files needed in the same folder
- orchestrator.py
- simple_mutation_test.py
- buggy_code.py
- test_buggy_code.py

## 5. Run
```
python orchestrator.py --source buggy_code.py --tests test_buggy_code.py
```

## What you should see
1. pytest runs, finds 2 failures in validate_password
2. Diagnosis Agent identifies the root cause(s)
3. Fix Agent patches buggy_code.py
4. pytest re-runs — passes (or retries if the fix was incomplete)
5. Mutation Re-check runs automatically using simple_mutation_test.py:
   generates mutants by flipping operators/constants in your AST, reruns
   pytest against each one, reports killed vs survived
6. Mutation Re-check Agent interprets the results and gives a final verdict

## Why not mutmut?
mutmut does not support native Windows (requires WSL). Rather than force a
WSL install, this project uses a small custom mutation engine built on
Python's ast module — verified to produce real, correct mutation results
on this exact codebase (5/5 mutants killed on the fixed validate_password).

## After it runs
Paste the full terminal output back to Claude to interpret the results.